## 1 Introduction

The standard model for the formation of millisecond pulsars MSPs is spin-up (recycling) of the neutron star by accretion in a low-mass X-ray binary (LMXB) system (Alpar et al., 1982; Radhakrishnan & Srinivasan, 1982), followed by turn-on of the radio pulsar mechanism after accretion stops. Of the 3319 pulsars in version 1.67 of the ATNF catalog1(Manchester, 2005), 501 have spin periods <20 ms and magnetic dipole field strengths B_{s}<10^{10} G, a combination that is a reliable indicator of a recycled origin.

Footnote 1: [https://www.atnf.csiro.au/research/pulsar/psrcat/](https://www.atnf.csiro.au/research/pulsar/psrcat/)

The Large Area Telescope on the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope has detected a significant fraction of known and new millisecond pulsars2. A major result is the profusion of new black widow and redbacks MSPs (collectively "spiders"), previously rare compact binaries having orbital periods \\lesssim 1 day, in which the relativistic pulsar wind interacts with a low-mass or substellar companion to heat its photosphere and help drive a wind. Black widows have degenerate companions of <0.05\\,M_{\\odot}, while redbacks have nondegenerate 0.1-1\\,M_{\\odot} companions that are hotter and of lower density than main-sequence stars of the same mass. These were recognized as distinct subclasses when a bimodal mass distribution became apparent among the Fermi MSP identifications (Roberts, 2011, 2013). Hui & Li (2019) catalogud 44 black widow pulsars (27 in the Galactic field and 17 in globular clusters), and 26 redbacks (14 in the Galactic field and 12 in globular clusters).

Footnote 2: [https://confluence.slac.stanford.edu/display/GLAMCOG/Public+List+of+LAT-Detected+Gamma-Ray+Pulsars](https://confluence.slac.stanford.edu/display/GLAMCOG/Public+List+of+LAT-Detected+Gamma-Ray+Pulsars)

Spider companions are close to filling their Roche lobes (Draghis et al., 2019; Strader et al., 2019), and their winds often obscure the radio pulsar signal for part of the orbit (e.g., Camilo et al., 2016; Deneva et al., 2016; Clark et al., 2021; Corongiu et al., 2021), making their timing difficult. The winds are sometimes seen in optical emission lines, principally H\\alpha(Halpern et al., 2017; Strader et al., 2019), but also He i(Romani et al., 2015). Nonthermal shock emission from the collision of the pulsar wind and the stellar wind is evident in X-rays (Bogdanov et al., 2005, 2011, 2014; Al Noori et al., 2018), and modulated in orbital phase. Photospheric heating is often strong, attributed to either high-energy photons from the shock or direct bombardment by pulsar wind particles (Romani & Sanchez, 2016; Sanchez & Romani, 2017). The optical modulation can be dominated by the contrast between the heated and "dark" sides of the companion (Breton et al., 2013; Schroeder & Halpern, 2014), or, if the heating effect is small, by ellipsoidal modulation of the light from the tidally distorted star, and starspots (Li et al., 2014; Bellm et al., 2016; van Staden & Antoniadis, 2016). All of these effects can change on timescales of minutes to months. Variable heating is seen in the optical light curves over weeks and months, while rapid flares with time scales of minutes increase the luminosity by a factor of 10 or more in X-rays, and up to \\sim 1 mag in optical (Halpern et al., 2017; Cho et al., 2018; An et al., 2017).

Some redbacks have an accretion disk producing double-peaked optical emission lines, but one that is truncated by the rotating pulsar magnetosphere or pulsar wind. They also display a unique pattern of "moding" in the X-ray and optical (Bogdanov et al., 2015; Bogdanov & Halpern, 2015; de Martino et al., 2015). Known as transitional millisecond pulsars (tMSPs) in the subluminous disk state, the majority were identified from Fermi sources. tMSPs may be the evolutionary link between the redback MSPs and their LMXB progenitors. There are additional tMSP candidates whose spin periods are not known because they have not yet been seen in a radio pulsar state. See Papitto & de Martino (2022) for a review of tMSPs, their accretion physics, and their relation to redbacks.

The subject of this Letter is the probable counterpart of 4FGL J0935.3+0901, which Wang et al. (2020) identified as a Swift X-ray source and a 2.47\\pm 0.04 hr optical photometric binary with a modulation of semiamplitude 0.30 mag. Strong, double-peaked optical emission lines were also detected. Wang et al. (2020) interpreted these properties as consistent with either a redback MSP or a tMSP.

The position of this star is (J2000) R.A.=09{}^{\\rm h}35{}^{m}20\\fs719, decl.=+09{}^{\\circ}00{}^{\\prime}35{}^{\\prime\\prime}.90 in the Gaia extended third data release (EDR3; Brown et al., 2021). Significant parallax is not detected, although proper motion components are measured: (\\mu_{\\alpha}\\cos\\delta,\\mu_{\\delta})=(-7.6\\pm 1.8,-2.9\\pm 1.1) mas yr{}^{-1}. Magnitudes in Pan-STARRS g,r,and i range from 20-21.5 (Wang et al., 2020).

Zheng et al. (2022) followed up with an X-ray light curve and spectrum of 4FGL J0935.3+0901 from XMM-Newton, which they conclude favors a redback in the radio pulsar state rather than a tMSP. They also conducted a 20 minute pulsar search with the Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical radio Telescope (FAST), which did not result in a detection. However, this observation covered only a small fraction of the orbit at an as yet unknown phase.

Here I present more extensive optical time-series photometry that reveals frequent variability and fast flaring behavior, providing additional evidence of interacting of a pulsar with the companion star. Section 2 describes the data and derives an ephemeris from time-series photometry at MDM Observatory, supplemented with data from the Zwicky Transient Facility (ZTF; Bellm et al., 2019). Section 3 discusses further implications of these results for the nature of the companion star and its interaction with the putative pulsar. Conclusions and suggestions for further work are presented in Section 4.

## 2 Observations and Analysis

### MDM 1.3m

I used the MDM Observatory 1.3 m McGraw-Hill telescope for time-series photometry. The thinned, backside-illuminated CCD "templeton" was windowed to achieve an efficient read/prep cycle time of 3 s compared with the 200 s exposure time. Given the small telescope aperture and faint magnitude of the target, typically in the range 20-21, I used only a single broadband (3200-6500 A) BG38 filter to optimize signal to noise, sacrificing absolute calibration and color information. To create the light curves, differential photometry was performed with respect to a nearby comparison star. An approximate magnitude was assigned to the comparison star from the average of its B and R in the second Palomar Sky Survey.

A log of the observations is given in Table 1. Figure 1 shows the eight nights of photometry, and a Lomb-Scargle periodogram of the combined nights that identifies the period (listed in line 1 of Table 2). The time system used is Barycentric Dynamical Time (TDB), as implemented by Eastman et al. (2010). Broad modulation of up to 1 mag on the 2.44 hr period is seen, resembling the heating light curves of redback and black widow companions. Its amplitude is too large to be due to tidal distortion of a Roche-lobe filling companion. There is also significant night-to-night variation of the amplitude and shape of the light curve.

